# Upstream fixtures are pinned by SHA256 against a specific upstream commit,
# and tests/test_review_workflow.py asserts that pin on every run. The whole
# point of those files is that they are byte-identical to what the upstream
# project publishes, so that a parser is tested against real tool output rather
# than against a copy someone tidied.
#
# Git's end-of-line translation breaks exactly that guarantee, and silently.
# TB-Profiler's example_collate.txt genuinely contains CRLF upstream; checked
# in without this rule, it was normalised to LF on commit, so the stored blob
# hashed 0b397374... while the pin and the real upstream file hash ccd745a9...
# Every Linux checkout then failed the hash assertion while Windows passed,
# because only Windows got the CRLF back on checkout.
#
# -text disables translation in both directions: these bytes are data, not
# source, and must round-trip unchanged on every platform.
tests/fixtures/upstream/** -text
